so let's return now to our top story  here this morning the 15th and 20th of  December are the dates that members of  the Royal College of Nursing will walk  out over pay the biggest strike in NHS  history so let's speak to a member of  the Union's executive I'm joined Now by  The Royal College of nursing's director  for England Patricia Marquis very good  morning to you Patricia Marquez thanks  so much for talking to us so the first  ever National walkout by your union a  big decision why have you taken it  well our members are sending a very loud  message to the government that things  need to change for nurses in their NHS  so we got a pay award this year of 1400  pounds which may sound a lot to people  but actually nursing pay has fallen  behind over the last 10 years by around  the rate of inflation plus five percent  so our members are hugely disappointed  that they didn't get anywhere near what  they needed to take their salaries back  to the level they were 10 years ago and  what's really worrying them is the  impact that that's having on patient  care there aren't enough nurses and  nursing staff to deliver the care that's  needed so patients are waiting too long  for their operations they're waiting for  ambulances they're waiting for beds and  it just is not safe for patients any  longer and nurses are saying we've had  enough we can't continue something needs  to change  and yet the health secretary for England  uh Steve Barkley would say that what  you're asking for effectively 19.2  percent of a pay rise costing 10 billion  pounds a year just simply isn't  affordable it's not realistic is it  so we we think it is realistic in terms  of the country needs to have more  nursing staff to be able to deliver the  services that the public need  so it's a choice that the government  needs to make but what we need to do now  is get back around the table and that's  the disappointment for us our members  sent this message a good few weeks ago  now to the health ministers and health  secretary and yet he's refused to talk  to us about the things that matter to  our members pay and patient safety and  really we can't we won't be able to move  this forward unless we do have  negotiations so we ask him again to come  back to the table to have some serious  negotiations about the things that need  to change to increase the number of  nurses in the workforce and increase the  amount and safety of care that people  can deliver  so if the health secretary Steve Barkley  has yet to meaningfully engage with you  over these negotiations what makes you  think that strike action will change  that  well I think we've tried talking and  talking and talking over years but the  government just hasn't listened and so  our members are telling us very loudly  now we can't just continue trying to  write letters and have conversations and  and they've they've given up that that's  going to work and we gave another few  weeks chance to see if things would  change with the signal of strikes with  the resounding message that the members  sent when they voted but that hasn't yet  worked and so it's with a really heavy  heart that we have had to now announce  dates in December when our members will  go on strike across the UK  well yes and how worried are you about  the impact on patients could people be  put in danger because of these strikes  well we found that patients are being  put in danger every single day and  actually when we take strike action we  will ensure that safety is is as it is  now so that patients are safe that there  is access to Emergency Services to make  sure that people  um you know life and limb services are  maintained but of course the strike is  going to be disruptive and we  acknowledge that but there is no point  in a striking unless it disrupts but  that disruption has got to send a strong  message to the government that they need  to talk and that we mean business and  that members mean business but we will  do it safely  well yes and talking of that disruption  this comes as there are seven million  people in England waiting for treatment  that's a record high this is only going  to make matters worse isn't it  I think we have to face the facts that a  day or two of strike action really is  not the issue the issue is years and  years of underfunding these waiting  lists have not grown to this level as a  result of nurses taking strike action  they've grown to this level in part  because of covid but largely over the  last 10 years with the underfunding the  reduction in the number of staff that  there are to deliver Services resulting  in increasing waiting lists increasing  waiting times  etc etc so no our strikes are not going  to make much difference to that the  problem is years of underfunding and the  action needs to happen now to improve  that  so what's your message to the patients  whose surgeries or treatments are going  to be canceled because of this strike  we're doing this for you this is about  nurses standing up for themselves but  also critically for patients  we know that the public support nursing  staff in general and we know they  support us through this action so we  want you to come on this journey with us  we are sorry for any disruption that's  caused but actually unless we do this we  don't see any Prospect of things  changing anytime soon  just a couple of questions on the the  process why did you choose to  regionalize the voting process was that  because you knew you didn't have the  numbers for a national vote  we think that members should have a  choice at local level and that's what  members have done they've taken that  choice at local level we also operate in  four countries across the UK and all  have different Health Systems and  different devolved responsibilities so  we decided to do that because of those  reasons and and as has been shown there  is stronger feeling in some areas than  others but even in the areas that didn't  get over the 50 levels set by the  government the numbers were huge in the  40 48 49 so we were very close and  members have spoken loudly across the  country even if they're not in a  position to take strike action they're  still sending that strong message where  people voted most times it was 80 90  percent of them voting yes to strike  and your strike one of one of the days  that you've named the 15th of December  comes in the same week as postal strikes  and Railway strikes has there been any  coordination with other unions over the  timing of your walkout  no there hasn't  um and in fact it may not look the same  if we were coordinating the reality is  that workers across the country are  sending a message to the government to  say that things that you know the salary  Rises that are being paid and the  threats to jobs in other sectors not for  us but in other sectors are really  having an impact so no there's not been  any coordination we are working as the  Royal College of Nursing on our own at  the moment  um and obviously other NHS unions sorry  Health unions are also currently  balloting their members so they may also  after Christmas be considering strike  action themselves  well Patricia Marquis a director for  England of the RCN thanks very much  indeed for talking to us here this  morning we appreciate it thank you
 

